      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by rewriting the equation in terms of the same base. We can use the property of exponents that states \\(a^m = b^n\\) implies \\(a = b^{mn}\\). Here, we can express both sides of the equation with the same base, but since 7 and 8 are not powers of each other, we need to use another approach.\n\nFirst, let's take the natural logarithm of both sides of the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we'll expand and rearrange the equation to isolate \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the left side:\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the right side by recognizing that \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8 = \\ln \\frac{7}{8}\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 8 - \\ln 7}\n\\]\n\nTo express \\(x\\) in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\), we need to rewrite the fraction in a more convenient form. Notice that \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8 = \\ln \\frac{7}{8}\\), so we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 8 - \\ln 7} = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\frac{8}{7}}\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that \\(\\ln \\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right) = \\ln a - \\ln b\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 8 - \\ln 7} = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\frac{8}{7}} = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\frac{8}{7}} = \\log_{\\frac{8}{7}} 7^7\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(b = \\frac{8}{7}\\). The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{8}{7}}\n\\]",
