      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by taking the natural logarithm of both sides. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nExpanding and rearranging the terms to isolate \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 - x \\ln 8 = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx (\\ln 7 - \\ln 8) = -7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we divide both sides by \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 7 - \\ln 8}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by recognizing that \\(\\ln 7 - \\ln 8 = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\). Thus, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-7 \\ln 7}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite the numerator as \\(-7 \\ln 7\\), which matches the form \\(\\ln b 7^7\\). Specifically, \\(\\ln b 7^7 = \\ln b + \\ln 7^7 = \\ln b + 7 \\ln 7\\). Therefore, comparing \\(\\ln b + 7 \\ln 7\\) with \\(\\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\), we see that:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b + 7 \\ln 7 = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\n\\]\n\nThis implies that:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right) - 7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that \\(a - b = \\ln a - \\ln b\\), we can rewrite the right-hand side as:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right) - \\ln (7^7)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8} \\cdot \\frac{1}{7^7}\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{8} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8^7}\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{7}{568435456}\\right)\n\\]\n\nExponentiating both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{7}{568435456}\n\\]\n\nHowever, we need to express \\(b\\) in the form \\(b = \\log_b 7^7\\). Using the property of logarithms \\(a = \\log_b a \\implies b^a = a\\), we can see that:\n\n\\[\nb = 7^{-7}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{7^{-7}}\n\\]",
